  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nAs the upper level ridge center remains over AZ/NM, the upper ridge \naxis will move eastward into the Northern and Central Plains on \nFriday. This will bring a noticeable increase in warm air advection, \nas H85 temperatures reach near 30C across the west. As surface low \npressure deepens over swrn SD, a windy south to south southwest wind \n20 to 35 mph can be expected. Highs to climb to the low 90s near and \nwest of Highway 83, and mid to upper 80s across the east. Stronger \nupper-level winds and disturbances are expected to focus across the \neastern Dakotas late Friday afternoon and evening. However, some of \nthis convection may develop as far south as the eastern half of \nnorth central and northeast Nebraska Friday night. \n\nSaturday and Sunday, the upper-level ridge is forecast to flatten, \nas an upper trough moves across the southern Canadian Prairies and \nfar Northern Plains. This will bring a quasi-zonal flow aloft and a \ndownsloping southwesterly surface wind and above normal \ntemperatures. Saturday will be the warmest day, reaching the mid 90s \nwest, with even some upper 90s possible southwest. A weak cold \nfrontal passage Saturday night, with low chances for showers/storms. \nSunday, highs slightly cooler in the upper 80s, with lower 90s \nsouthwest. Low chances again for showers/storms.\n\nA longwave trough moving through the West Coast of the U.S. will \nbring an increased southwesterly flow aloft. A surface front may \nremain stalled out across southern Kansas Monday and Tuesday, \nproviding a better chance for showers and storms Monday night, and \nagain Tuesday night. High temperatures moderate back into the mid \n80s to near 90. With stronger flow aloft overlapping ample \ninstability, the pattern may become supportive of severe weather \nchances. Widespread meaningful rainfall is not expected through the \nextended period. NBM 48 hour probabilities for at least 0.25\" ending \nSunday morning is only 15 percent across the far northeast and \nincreases to 15 to 25 percent area wide for the 48 hour period \nending Tuesday morning.",
